Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from the story "Nick and Me"
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. PETER'S SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

PETER (30s), clean-cut and thoughtful, sits at a wooden desk overlooking a foggy Seattle skyline. He writes in a leather journal.

PETER
Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about that stupid book we had to read in junior high. No, I’m talking about my real Big Brother.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

1990s Milwaukee. A modest fifth-floor walk-up apartment.

NICK (20s), broad-shouldered with scarred eyebrows and wild reddish-brown hair, hauls six heavy grocery bags through the doorway in one trip.

PETER (18), thin and quiet, follows behind carrying two small bags.

MA (50s), frail with tired brown eyes, sits in an overstuffed chair in front of a flickering television set.

NICK
(setting bags down)
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill.

Peter nods silently, looking down at his shoes.

PETER
(NARRATOR V.O.)
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. A modern-day Viking. He took care of us after Dad took off when I was eight.

EXT. PIZZERIA - DAY

Peter walks his bicycle toward the entrance of a neighborhood pizza joint.

SIONNA (20s), slender with short brown hair, pale skin, and light blue eyes, trips on the uneven concrete sidewalk. She brushes into Peter's shoulder.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
(brushing off her shirt)
Yes.

Sionna wears a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a heavy backpack. A fish-symbol necklace rests against her collarbone.

PETER
I work here, I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

Peter stares into her light blue eyes, completely entranced.

PETER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her phone number on a small scrap of paper and hands it to him.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

A cozy, student-filled coffee shop. Textbooks and backpacks litter the small wooden tables.

Peter and Sionna sit across from each other at a corner table with two mugs of coffee.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
(smiling)
Yeah, I know. I read it. The love that Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him. A love that never changes will destroy you.

PETER
(NARRATOR V.O.)
She made me feel smarter just by being around her. Something inside of me was coming awake.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma stands in the kitchen, confused, staring at a line of pill bottles set up on the counter.

PETER
Ma, what’s the matter?

MA
I can’t find my pain pills, I know I put them here somewhere. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, what are you talking about? We don’t go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you in the box for today.

Ma looks at Peter with empty, glassy eyes, then blinks and snaps back to reality.

MA
Oh, right, I am so sorry. I see them right here. I don't know what got into me.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

A crowded, smoky bar filled with pool tables, loud rock music, and beer signs.

Nick sits at the bar, drinking a Jack and Coke. Peter sits beside him.

NICK
What’s shakin’, little brother? I can tell you’re hidin’ something. What’s goin’ on?

PETER
Ma was all confused today. She thought we went to church.

Nick’s face flushes dark red. He slams his glass on the bar.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day! Why don’t you take her to the store or something? Holy Christ, do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart!

PETER
I just needed to get away from her for a while, that's all.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro. You just might learn something.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

A stone municipal office.

Peter sits across a wooden desk from OFFICER DUPUIS (50s), a stern detective in uniform.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Mr. Olson, last night about eight p.m., your brother's vehicle was hit broadside at the intersection of Werner and Lowe. I regret to tell you that your mother passed away at the scene.

Peter’s hands tremble around a paper cup of water.

PETER
And... Nick?

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition at the hospital. Toxicology is pending. We suspect he was driving under the influence.

PETER
(NARRATOR V.O.)
Drunk driving. He got drunk to take Ma to church, and he killed her.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

A warm, modest living room. Sionna sits on a green suede couch, reading a library book.

A heavy, violent KNOCK rattles the front door. Before Sionna can rise, NICK kicks the door open.

Nick limps heavily, his face bruised and yellowed, his reddish hair wild. He holds his right arm stiffly.

SIONNA
(alarmed)
Nick? Is that you?

NICK
Yeah, it’s me. I want to talk to you, hippie chick.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally. What is on your mind?

NICK
(stepping close)
You’re the reason I’m probably going to jail. You ruined my life! You confused my brother's stupid brain with a bunch of crazy ideas about school!

Nick grabs Sionna’s neck with his left hand, forcing her back against the hallway wall. Sionna struggles, gasping for air.

NICK
You stay the fuck away from my brother.

Nick pulls a Swiss Army knife from his coat pocket with his right hand and flicks the blade open near her face.

NICK
No more contact. You got that?

The front door swings wide open. Peter bursts into the hallway, holding his bicycle helmet.

PETER
Holy shit, Nick! What the fuck are you doing?!

Peter lunges forward and grabs Nick’s coat sleeve.

Nick thrusts his elbow backward, striking Peter square in the face. In the chaotic struggle, Nick's knife hand slashes across Sionna's shoulder.

Sionna cries out and sinks down the wall, blood spreading across her shirt.

Nick looks down at the blood, panics, wipes the blade on her sleeve, and bolts out the front door.

Peter drops to his knees beside Sionna, pressing both hands hard against her wound.

PETER
Sionna! Hold on! Hold on!

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY (MONTHS LATER)

Winter sunlight filters through the window.

Sionna sits in a rocking chair, her left shoulder bound in a support brace. Her face is pale and expressionless.

Peter sits on the edge of the couch opposite her.

PETER
Sionna, please talk to me. You know Nick is in prison now. He can't hurt you anymore.

SIONNA
(staring at the floor)
I can't feel myself anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. I need to go stay with my parents.

PETER
Sionna, please...

SIONNA
(softly)
I love you, Peter. But I can't.

Peter buries his face in his hands as Sionna slowly turns and walks down the dark hallway.

INT. MILWAUKEE DELI - DAY (YEARS LATER)

Present day. Peter (now 30s, dressed in a sharp coat) sits eating a sandwich at a window booth.

BOB (30s), thicker around the middle with thinning gray hair, sits at a neighboring table eating a burger. Bob looks up and gasps.

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro?

PETER
Bob?

Bob leaps up and slaps Peter hard on the back.

BOB
Look at you, man! Look at you! Big shoulders, Ivy League talk. Are you back to see Nick?

PETER
Yeah, Bob. I thought it was time.

BOB
(nodding solemnly)
Good for you, man. Let the shit go. Let it go.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION - VISITING ROOM - DAY

A sterile room divided by thick glass and small wall-mounted yellow telephones.

Peter picks up the receiver.

On the other side of the glass, NICK enters wearing a faded orange prison jumpsuit. He is thin, graying at the temples, and walks with a slight limp. He sits and picks up his phone.

NICK
(weakly)
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick.

NICK
It's looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe as early as two more years for my parole hearing.

PETER
I'm here to tell you something, Nick. I have spent the last eight years thinking and growing. I've educated myself, and I came to realize a few things. You're an asshole. You killed our mother, and you tried to kill the love of my life. I'll never see you again because of you.

Nick looks down at the shelf, tears welling in his bloodshot eyes.

PETER
But I have to add this, and this is the most important part: I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears stream down Nick's weathered cheeks, dripping onto the counter.

NICK
I'm so sorry, man. I'm so sorry. You're right. You're a big man, and I'm a small one.

PRISON GUARD (O.S.)
Time's up, Olson.

Nick wipes his face and looks up at Peter with genuine pride.

NICK
What are you doing now, Peter?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We're expanding.

NICK
(smiling through tears)
President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I love you, man.

PETER
I love you too, Nick. Goodbye.

Nick hangs up his phone and is escorted down the corridor by the guard. Peter slowly hangs up his receiver and smiles.

INT. PETER'S SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

Peter sits at his wooden desk in Seattle, holding his leather journal. He looks out the window at the rain, serene and completely at peace.

PETER
(NARRATOR V.O.)
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, or judgment.

Peter closes the journal and sets down his pen.

PETER
(whispering to himself)
President Peter Olson. That's me.
